{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../../../AGENTS.md | sed -n '91,119p'; nl -ba PAID-DESIGN.md | sed -n '68,147p;168,205p'; nl -ba codex-r26.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../../../AGENTS.md | sed -n '91,119p'; nl -ba PAID-DESIGN.md | sed -n '68,147p;168,205p'; nl -ba codex-r26.md\"","aggregated_output":"    91\t## 2. Before writing code: design\n    92\t\n    93\tCode written before the thinking costs rounds of review. A design holds these tables before code and before any review; a blank cell\n    94\tmeans it is not ready:\n    95\t\n    96\t1. **State × event**, for work that spans more than one step, run or day: every state against every event it can meet (success,\n    97\t   refusal, a refused retry, an external failure, a crash midway, work still in flight when a run ends, a later run on a later date,\n    98\t   configuration changed in between, an input corrected while waiting, another writer of the same data, two scopes at once), including\n    99\t   the work before its first step and after its terminal outcome. Each cell names the transition, what is written, and the date every\n   100\t   read and write uses.\n   101\t2. **Value × reader.** Every value a change writes, against every reader of it (found by searching the field) and every input source the\n   102\t   value can come from. A rule over a kind of data lists every writer of that kind. A change to how often something fires is counted\n   103\t   against every reader of its output.\n   104\t3. **New path × old checks.** For a new input, derived value, mode or changed data shape: every check the old path passes, and whether\n   105\t   the new one meets it. A changed shape re-derives every check written for the old one.\n   106\t4. **Prior decisions.** The decisions searched for the mechanism by name; any decision that rejected or bounded it is quoted in the\n   107\t   design and in the question to Alex.\n   108\t\n   109\t- A contract over text or shapes states the closed set of what passes (a full match); anything else fails or is not judged. Enumerate\n   110\t  the accepted set against every context and reader it meets before the first edit.\n   111\t- **Code owns identity, never importance.** Before building a closed list, enum or gate, write which decision it takes from the model\n   112\t  and whether that decision is identity (\"are these the same thing?\") or judgement (\"does this matter?\"); build only the identity part.\n   113\t  A list of what matters is at most a hint shown to the model (Alex, 2026-10-25: \"你这个做法难道不是非常的限死吗？永远都是一堆财务指标，真正的观点呢？\").\n   114\t- **No case-fitted fix.** A hand list, a special path or a rule fitted to the case in view fixes that case and breaks the next. First ask\n   115\t  whether the judge had the material and the definition it needed, and whether a typed field is missing; run the proposal against the\n   116\t  existing tests and the gold for a counter-example before building (D167: \"我不要 hardcode 解法 it's premature optimization, not general root fix\").\n   117\t- A blind review measures the tables. A serious finding they do not hold shows a missing dimension: add it and walk every row again,\n   118\t  never answer the finding alone. Code starts when the review finds nothing serious. A design whose core contract is still open is a\n   119\t  question, not something to build.\n    68\t\n    69\t## 4. Reading rule (frozen before the send)\n    70\t\n    71\t**Unit:** a calendar date of the fixed window 07-10..09-20 (73 dates); on each, each question's conclusion is its last accepted\n    72\tjudgement on or before the date, carried forward. A date's class comes from the **distinct answer pair** (v9's and v14's answers) in\n    73\tforce that day.\n    74\t\n    75\t**Hand classification of every distinct pair (round 23):** `contradictions.py` orders the work (definite and ambiguous first); every\n    76\tdistinct pair in all six sequences is read by hand against both answers and classed **definite** (Data Center's annual low end above\n    77\ttotal revenue's high end, as each answer finally states its FY2027 forecast), **overlap**, **clear**, or **unresolved** (an answer\n    78\tstates no FY2027 forecast that can be read). A parser class that the reading changes is recorded as a misparse with its corrected\n    79\tclass. Dates inherit their pair's class. Before reading, the pairs are listed per sequence with their date spans.\n    80\t\n    81\t**Primary line, P, per pair n (round 24: three outcomes):** over all 73 dates, each arm's dates are definite (D), not definite\n    82\t(overlap or clear, N) or unresolved (U), each date keeping its own arm's class even where the other arm is unresolved. Linked-n's\n    83\tdefinite count lies between D_L and D_L + U_L; control-n's between D_C and D_C + U_C.\n    84\t- **Improved:** D_L + U_L < D_C (the least favourable completion still has fewer definite dates in the linked arm).\n    85\t- **Not improved:** D_L >= D_C + U_C (even the most favourable completion cannot give the linked arm fewer).\n    86\t- **Undecided:** otherwise (the unresolved dates could change the result).\n    87\t- **Testable:** D_C >= 2 (known definite dates of the control over all 73, not only dates readable in both arms).\n    88\t- **Readable:** each arm has at least 60 of the 73 dates resolved **in the hand-read classes** (before any credit adjustment): a\n    89\t  coverage policy that deliberately allows at most 13 unreadable dates per arm (82.2%). A readable forecast with unsupported arithmetic\n    90\t  stays readable; its uncertainty reaches P through the credit-adjusted classes the bounds use.\n    91\t- **Credit (rounds 24–25; one rule):** a **gain** is a date on which the linked arm is not definite and the control is definite. Only\n    92\t  gains are ever adjusted; no other date's class changes for credit. When a pair is provisionally improved, **every** gain is reviewed:\n    93\t  both linked answers in force on it (v9's and v14's), deduplicated by judgement id, are read for July's rule B (arithmetic following\n    94\t  from stated inputs; each assumption that sizes the result disclosed and supported) and for H4. A gain whose forecasts carry a material\n    95\t  rule-B failure on which its reconciliation rests earns no credit (counted definite for P). A gain resting on an unsupported CPU\n    96\t  increment: when the increment can be quantified, the forecast with it removed gives the gain's credit-adjusted class; when it cannot,\n    97\t  the gain counts unresolved: **every** such gain of the pair is marked unresolved, and the bounds are then recomputed once across the\n    98\t  pair (round 26: two gains marked one at a time could each look harmless). Each date keeps its actual class in the report beside its\n    99\t  credit-adjusted class; P is recomputed on the credit-adjusted classes. A confirmed CPU double count stays a harm (H4) whatever its\n   100\t  date.\n   101\t- Reported apart, per arm: definite dates that became overlap and those that became clear.\n   102\t\n   103\t**Harm lines (each checked on all three pairs):**\n   104\t- **H1, refusals (round 24):** on **matched reader-days** (a reader judged on that day in both arms of the pair), the attempted reader\n   105\t  judgements and the model refusals among them (first attempts the code refused, terminal refusals included); refusals on linked-only\n   106\t  propagation days reported apart. Harm at **4 or more** refusals above its control on matched reader-days (3 extra is tolerated).\n   107\t  Rationale, frozen before the run: old-2's stored run refused 4 of 1,616 judgements (0.25%), about 0.6 expected per ~250 reader-days, so\n   108\t  4 extra is far above that base rate while up to 3 stays inside ordinary variation. A **linked double refusal** (the corrected answer\n   109\t  refused again: `judgement_refused_twice` stops the day) is a product failure: the verdict is **fail** whatever else holds; a control\n   110\t  double refusal makes the comparison invalid.\n   111\t- **H2, the fixed trajectory:** an unexplained difference in a non-reader request or its substantive output makes the comparison\n   112\t  **invalid** (not a harm: the paired design no longer holds).\n   113\t- **H3, sourcing through the link (round 23):** a figure an answer presents as the market's (consensus, guidance, an analyst estimate)\n   114\t  is followed through the exact request: through the dependency block to the read judgement that stated it, and from there to its dated\n   115\t  outside statement (a company figure such as Bloomberg's $393B total-revenue estimate reaching Data Center through total revenue is the\n   116\t  intended use, if it is named as a company figure). A grounding error: a figure supported only by the system's own forecast, or a\n   117\t  company estimate presented as a segment consensus. Checked regardless of keywords: every reader reference whose figure differs from\n   118\t  that reader's previous reference in the sequence in **amount, scope or attribution** (round 24: an unchanged $393B can move from a\n   119\t  company figure to a segment consensus), and every reader answer on the seven fixed dates below; keyword hits elsewhere are read too.\n   120\t  Harm when a linked sequence has a grounding error and its control's conclusion in force on that date (its fresh judgement, or the one\n   121\t  carried forward) does not have the same specific error.\n   122\t- **H4, the CPU link:** every post-link v14 answer in the linked sequences is read against the exact CPU conclusion it received and\n   123\t  its control's v14 answer that day. Double counting the CPU forecast is harm. Its effect on P goes only through the credit rule above.\n   124\t- **H5, arithmetic and sourcing (July's rule B):** v9 and v14 at seven fixed dates (07-12, 07-26, 08-09, 08-23, 09-06, 09-13, 09-20) in\n   125\t  all six sequences; the unit is a question-date; Claude reads first, a blind reader second; a disagreement goes to a third,\n   126\t  source-backed read whose class decides. H5 is a separate paired harm count: a linked sequence with at least 2 more material failures\n   127\t  than its control is harm. Its effect on P goes only through the credit rule above (a shared error moves no date).\n   128\t\n   129\t**Verdict precedence:** (1) a **linked double refusal** → fail; (2) **invalid or incomplete** (an infrastructure stop: budget, transport\n   130\tor harness; a control's double refusal; an H2 difference; an arm under 60 resolved dates) → inconclusive; (3) **confirmed harm** (H1, H3,\n   131\tH4 double count, H5) → fail; (4) **untestable** (a control with D_C < 2) → inconclusive, \"the class did not recur\"; (5) **P**: all three\n   132\tpairs improved → pass; any pair not improved → fail; otherwise (some pair undecided) → inconclusive.\n   133\t\n   134\t**The worksheet (pinned before the launch; round 24):** built by `worksheet.py` from the six stores and archives after the run, its\n   135\tformat and code reviewed and run on the dry outputs before the send. One row per distinct v9/v14 answer pair per sequence, keyed by\n   136\tboth judgement ids and the pair's date span; it carries both answers' full `implied_outcome` and reasons, and fields for the reader:\n   137\tthe FY2027 low and high ends as each answer finally states them, the excerpt they come from, the class, a note. H3, H4 and H5 rows carry\n   138\tthe exact request text (dependency block marked), the read judgement each figure came through, and the dated source quotes. Worked\n   139\texample (old-1 09-08): Data Center \"$419 billion for FY2027\" against total revenue \"$414.3 billion ... supports roughly $414–421\n   140\tbillion\": low end 419 is not above high end 421, so **overlap**, though the parser took $414.3B and flagged it definite. Claude reads\n   141\tfirst; a blind reader (Codex, labels shuffled, key kept outside the repository) second; every disagreement that could change a pair's\n   142\toutcome, an H3 or H4 finding, or an H5 mark goes to a third, source-backed read whose class decides. Reading load on old-2's schedule:\n   143\tabout 72 distinct pairs a sequence (up to 438 rows, each read twice: 876 classifications), about 213 linked v14 reviews (twice where\n   144\tan H4 finding is made), 168 H5 marks (84 question-dates twice), the H3 traces, and the full gain review (both supplying answers of every\n   145\tgain, deduplicated), before adjudication; the dry review counts each reader's rows apart; estimated 8–12 hours of reading across the\n   146\treaders, apart from the run's three hours.\n   147\t\n   168\t   §3 items 2–3. 2. P on annual ranges, carry-forward, coverage, all three pairs, outcomes for every case, the parser validated on\n   169\t   flagged and unflagged stored answers: §4, `parser-validation.md`. 3. H4 on every post-link v14 answer; H3 traced outside the\n   170\t   dependency block; H5 with an effect on the verdict; per-link report; refusals with a denominator, stops apart: §4. 4. July's latch:\n   171\t   §1, §2, §6.\n   172\tRound 23: freshness on the archive side, admission bound to the judging day, the upstream snapshot at prompt construction with its three\n   173\tcases: §2, §3 item 2. Every distinct pair hand-classified, misparses recorded, carried-forward dates: §4. Paired rates on jointly\n   174\treadable dates of the fixed 73, unresolved dates unable to reverse, conversions to overlap reported apart: §4 P. H3 traced through the\n   175\tlink to the outside statement: §4 H3. H1 second refusal, H2 invalidates, H4 and H5 consequences in P, disagreement resolution,\n   176\tverdict precedence: §4. Time: §6.\n   177\tRound 24: three outcomes per pair from two-sided bounds over all 73 dates, the floor on known control dates, 60 stated as a policy: §4 P.\n   178\tProduct against infrastructure failure, matched reader-days, the H1 tolerance's rationale: §4 H1 and precedence. Credit only for gains\n   179\tthat pass rule B, the CPU increment shown removed or the pair undecided, H3 triggers on amount, scope or attribution against the\n   180\tcontrol's conclusion in force: §4. The worksheet, its worked example, adjudication for P, H3, H4 and H5, reading time: §4.\n   181\tRound 25: every gain of a provisionally improved pair reviewed, both supplying answers, deduplicated by judgement id; only gains\n   182\tadjusted, by one credit rule; H4 and H5 reach P only through it; H1's boundary stated (harm at 4); reading load counted per reader:\n   183\t§4. The harness review will run two $0 verdict checks: a pair whose control has 2 definite dates and the linked arm none (both gains\n   184\treviewed, withdrawing both gives a tie: not improved); a material error shared by both arms on a clear date (no change to P); the same\n   185\tpair with both gains resting on unquantifiable CPU increments (both marked unresolved together: D_L + U_L = 2, not below D_C = 2:\n   186\tundecided). Round 26: every unquantifiable CPU gain marked unresolved before one recomputation; coverage on the hand-read classes, the\n   187\tbounds on the credit-adjusted ones: §4.\n     1\t**NOT READY.** Version 5 fixes both round-25 counterexamples. One ambiguity remains in how the credit rule handles multiple unquantifiable CPU gains. Two readers can still reach **pass** and **inconclusive** from the same answers. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:89)\n     2\t\n     3\t### Round-25 findings\n     4\t\n     5\tBoth fixes hold. Every gain in a provisionally improved pair now gets Rule B review of both supplying judgements. If the control has two definite dates and both linked gains lose credit, the result is a tie. Only gain dates can be adjusted, so a material error shared by both arms on a clear date leaves P unchanged. H4 double counting and the H5 sample retain their separate harm checks. Both verdict checks are specified in §7. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:89) · [H4 and H5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:119) · [Verdict checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:178)\n     6\t\n     7\tH1 now explicitly tolerates three excess refusals and fails at four. The worksheet counts the two reads separately and includes H3 and the full gain review. Those findings are closed. [H1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:100) · [Reading load](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:131)\n     8\t\n     9\t### Exact remaining change\n    10\t\n    11\tRemove the per-gain condition **“and could decide the pair.”** Suppose a control has two definite dates and the linked arm has none, with both gains resting on unquantifiable unsupported CPU increments. Marking either gain unresolved by itself still leaves an improvement, since **1 < 2**. Marking both unresolved makes the pair **undecided**, since **2 < 2** is false. Mark **every** such gain unresolved, then recompute the bounds once across the pair. [Bounds](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:80) · [CPU credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:93)\n    12\t\n    13\tState that the **60-date coverage check uses the original hand-read classes**, while the bounds use the credit-adjusted classes. A readable forecast with unsupported arithmetic still counts as readable, and its uncertainty still affects P. Add the two-unquantifiable-gains case to the $0 verdict checks. [Coverage rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:86) · [Verdict precedence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:126)","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
