{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba PAID-DESIGN.md | sed -n '1,69p'; rg -n 'ambigu|clear' contradictions.py\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# The paid whole-window comparison of poc-v120 against fresh controls: design, version 6 (D699, D705, D706; for council review)\n     2\t\n     3\tVersion 1 was reviewed in council R round 22 (`codex-r22.md`, NOT READY: four changes), version 2 in round 23 (`codex-r23.md`, NOT\n     4\tREADY: the reading rule and the freshness proof), version 3 in round 24 (`codex-r24.md`, NOT READY: three outcomes per pair, product\n     5\tagainst infrastructure failure, decisive gains traced, the worksheet), version 4 in round 25 (`codex-r25.md`, NOT READY: one rule for\n     6\tcredit), version 5 in round 26 (`codex-r26.md`, NOT READY: unquantifiable CPU gains marked together; coverage on the read classes); each\n     7\tfinding is answered below (§7).\n     8\t\n     9\t## 0. What it decides, and what it can reach\n    10\t\n    11\tIt decides whether linking (poc-v120: Jev's link questions version 2; judging-v8's fuller dependency block) makes the linked number\n    12\tquestions' judgements agree over the release window without harm elsewhere: the evidence for Alex's choice whether the linking version\n    13\treplaces poc-v118 (D699). It is paired on old-2's trajectory (DESIGN.md §1): every request byte-equal to one old-2 sent is answered with\n    14\told-2's answer; only the readers' judgements are bought.\n    15\t\n    16\t**The attainable verdict ($0):** Data Center FY2027 above total revenue FY2027 (`contradictions.py`, calendar dates 07-01..09-20,\n    17\tparser second version, before hand reading): old-1 11 days of 77, old-2 56 of 73, old-3 1 of 33 (that one a misparse,\n    18\t`parser-validation.md`). The class occurs and varies widely between draws; old-2's stored path is an extreme and not a fair control.\n    19\tA linked sequence that reconciles would show near zero; only fresh controls can serve as the baseline.\n    20\t\n    21\t## 1. Arms (paired by draw n = 1, 2, 3)\n    22\t\n    23\t- **linked-n:** poc-v120 from an empty store on its own copy of old-2's archive. Its link jobs are answered only by probe draw n's\n    24\t  answers, matched by request hash (`window_test.probe_answers`; any other link job stops the sequence before a purchase), so its links\n    25\t  are probe draw n's proposals, now written. Readers in all three draws: v9 (Data Center FY2027, reads v14), v14 (total revenue FY2027,\n    26\t  reads v8), v16 (total CPU FY2027, reads v14), v21 (revenue growth FY2027, reads v16, and v9 in draws 2 and 3).\n    27\t- **control-n:** poc-v118 from an empty store on its own copy of old-2's archive, its readers' old judgements retired as below.\n    28\t- **Fresh judgements, delimited by date (round 22):** a reader's link is written by an article of day D_R (v9, v14: 07-10, seq 148;\n    29\t  v16: seq 265's day; v21: seq 534's day); its first affected judgement is its first judgement on a day at or after D_R (old-2: seq 167,\n    30\t  274, 542). Every judgement of a reader on a day at or after D_R must be a fresh answer in both arms: in linked-n because its request\n    31\t  carries the dependency block, in control-n because the old request family is retired. The old family of a reader is identified by\n    32\t  reader and judgement day: every archive judge row of that reader (accepted answers, first attempts the stored run refused, and their\n    33\t  retries) whose judgement day is at or after D_R; archive rows carry no day, so a row is assigned to a day through old-2's\n    34\t  `judgements` table (accepted rows) and through its request text (the refused first attempts, whose request equals the retry's before\n    35\t  the correction lines). Every retired row is listed before the send and checked against old-2's judgements by reader and day.\n    36\t- **old-2's stored path:** a labelled historical baseline, outside the fresh-control figures.\n    37\t- All six sequences run at once, one paid call at a time within each (July's latch, round 22), one shared reservation ledger at the\n    38\t  ceiling Alex names.\n    39\t\n    40\t## 2. Harness rules (July's, `dependency-design/july_test.py`, extended to the window)\n    41\t\n    42\t- Harness-only marker, the store lock, never resumed; any stop is final and makes the comparison inconclusive.\n    43\t- **Allow-list before the budget transport, per sequence, under July's latch** (admission and the inner call under one lock; the first\n    44\t  refusal closes the sequence): a linked sequence may buy only judge requests of its readers on days at or after their D_R; a control\n    45\t  only the retired readers' judge requests on those days; a link job only from the probe draw. Admission is bound to the replay's\n    46\t  judging day (the harness wraps `round.judge_day` to know the day of every judge request), not to the request's text. Any other request that misses the archive\n    47\t  stops the sequence (round 22 found no legitimate non-reader change on this trajectory). The four answers old-2's own run refused are\n    48\t  given back once each for non-readers, as in the `neither` pass.\n    49\t- Settlement at published rates with cache writes at 1.25x; reservation from request size (D696).\n    50\t\n    51\t## 3. The $0 proofs before the send\n    52\t\n    53\t1. `v120-replay` (done): poc-v120 with each probe draw's own answers meets all 17 link jobs and ends equal to old-2.\n    54\t2. **Linked dry run per draw** (a fake judge answering readers with markers): committed links equal probe draw n's proposals; no link\n    55\t   job outside the probe; no circle correction; every non-reader judgement equal to old-2's by question and day in its request and its\n    56\t   substantive fields (rung, reasons, reference, implied outcome, cites); **freshness checked on the archive side as well as the\n    57\t   transport side** (round 23: a cache hit bypasses the transport): every reader judgement made on a day at or after its D_R has a call\n    58\t   id above the copied archive's top, and none before; **the upstream snapshot:** when each reader prompt is built, the harness records\n    59\t   the judgement id, date, reference and forecast of each question it reads (wrapping `store.current_state` as `judging.build_prompt`\n    60\t   calls it) and checks the rendered dependency block against that snapshot, never against the final ledger; the cases a same-day\n    61\t   upstream update, a carried-forward upstream answer (old-2's v14 has ten judgement days with no v8 judgement that day) and a refused\n    62\t   upstream answer followed by its accepted retry are each exercised; the added days listed.\n    63\t3. **Control dry run per draw:** the retired rows listed and checked; only the retired readers reach the transport, on the right days;\n    64\t   every other judgement equal to old-2's by question and day.\n    65\t4. The stop paths and the latch race, on the window.\n    66\t5. Price from old-2's archived usage of the readers' days (round 22: 247 judgements, $0.872 a sequence) plus the dry runs' added days,\n    67\t   prompt growth from the dependency block, retries, and the live reservations, each shown apart.\n    68\t\n    69\t## 4. Reading rule (frozen before the send)\n10:  clear:     the segment's high end is at or below the company's low end;\n11:  ambiguous: either forecast fell to the last-amount rule with several distinct amounts (reported, read by hand);\n52:    2027\"); else an amount opening the text (within its first 30 characters); else the last amount, marked ambiguous when the text\n62:    return (*found[-1][2], 'last' if len({f[2] for f in found}) == 1 else 'ambiguous')\n68:    if 'ambiguous' in (segment[2], company[2]):\n69:        return 'ambiguous'\n73:        return 'clear'\n110:    counts = {name: sum(1 for r in rows if r[1] == name) for name in ('definite', 'overlap', 'clear', 'ambiguous', 'unparsed')}\n112:    counts['coverage'] = round((len(rows) - counts['unparsed'] - counts['ambiguous']) / len(rows), 3) if rows else 0.0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"**READY TO BUILD THE PAID HARNESS.** Both round-26 findings are fixed. I found no remaining ambiguity in the revised credit and coverage rules that would let the same adjudicated answers produce opposite verdicts.\n\n- **Unquantifiable CPU gains.** Mark every affected gain unresolved, then recompute the pair’s bounds once. With two definite control dates and two unquantifiable linked gains, the linked count can range from zero to two, so the pair is **undecided**. §7 records this as the third $0 verdict check. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:91) · [Verdict checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:183)\n- **Coverage.** The 60-date floor uses the original hand-read classes. The bounds use the credit-adjusted classes. I checked the boundary in memory: with 60 readable linked dates, marking one gain unresolved for credit leaves coverage at 60 and increases uncertainty in P. [Coverage and bounds](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:81)\n\nUse §4 as the verdict authority in the build. §2’s “any stop” shorthand should point to §4, which explicitly makes a linked double refusal a failure. The harness, worksheet builder, dry runs, and all three verdict checks return for review before any send. [Harness rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:40) · [Verdict precedence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:129)"}}
